{#
  Error pages — every status code the site can hand back, wearing the front door.

  ONE template on purpose. Craft resolves by status code first (`_views/404.twig`
  would win over this), and there is deliberately no such file: a 404 and a 500
  differ by one line of copy, not by a layout, and the failure mode of per-code
  templates is a 500 page nobody has looked at since the day it was written.

  It lives in here rather than loose at the templates root because
  `errorTemplatePrefix` is set to '_views/' in config/general.php. Craft's lookup is
  prefix + code, then prefix + 'offline' for a 503, then prefix + 'error' — so this
  file is the catch-all end of that chain.

  Craft hands over `statusCode` (a STRING, not an int — compare against '404'), plus
  `message`, `code`, `file` and `line`. See
  vendor/craftcms/cms/src/controllers/TemplatesController.php.

  `message` is deliberately NOT rendered. For a UserException it is the exception's
  own words and for anything else a flat "Server Error" — Craft's language rather
  than Jon's, always English whatever the site is, and free to name internals.

  Nothing here reads `entry`, because there isn't one. The layout, head.twig and
  scripts.twig all already guard for that (`entry is defined and entry`), which is
  why this needs no special handling — and why nothing added here later should
  assume a page element.

  NOTE ON LOCAL TESTING: with devMode on you will never see this. Craft serves Yii's
  own debug error view instead, so a 404 comes back as a bare "404 Not Found" page.
  Set CRAFT_DEV_MODE=false to check it, and put it back afterwards.
#}
{% extends '_layouts/page.twig' %}

{% set tplPath = _self %}

{# No slabModifier: the default is the olive p500 the homepage uses. An error is a
   front door, not a content page — the cream slab was tried and reads as an article
   about the mistake. #}

{# A line each, keyed by the code Craft resolved. The fallback covers everything not
   listed — there is no per-code sentence worth writing for a 502. No body copy and no
   eyebrow: the heading IS the page, and the code itself is already in the HTTP status
   where the thing that cares about it is looking. #}
{% set titles = {
    '404': 'Page not found',
    '403': 'Access required',
    '503': 'Website offline',
} %}

{% set title = titles[statusCode] ?? 'Something went wrong' %}

{# The tab reads "That page doesn't exist | Jon Leverrier" rather than the bare site
   name. pageTitleParts is head.twig's own override hook, so the site-name segment and
   the pagination prefix keep working. #}
{% set pageTitleParts = [title] %}

{% block siteHead %}
    {# The status code is what actually keeps a 404 out of an index, and Craft sends
       it. This is belt and braces for crawlers that are less careful, and for anything
       that reaches this template with a 200 by accident. #}
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex">
{% endblock %}

{% block siteContent %}
{#
  The homepage's front door, minus everything that asks a question.

  `data-frontdoor` IS REQUIRED, and not for the reason the name suggests. It looks
  like a Jonson hook — the door to warp through when a question is asked, the panel
  to hide when a thread is restored — but in app.js the point cloud's mount sits
  INSIDE `if (hero)`, and `hero` is `querySelector('[data-frontdoor]')`. Drop the
  attribute and `[data-hero]` renders an empty box: right size, no canvas, no error.
  The Jonson behaviours it also switches on are all inert here — thread-memory bails
  before it reaches the door (no `[data-jonson-view]` on this page), and the warp only
  fires on a question that can't be asked without a form.

  No ask bar. An error page is a dead end to get OUT of, not a place to start a
  conversation, and an input here reads as the site asking the visitor to do the work
  of finding what it lost. The header and the site footer carry the routes on.
#}
<section class="c-frontdoor" data-frontdoor>
    <div class="c-frontdoor__bg">
        {# No src — hero.js imports the binary as a bundled asset so the built
           filename carries a content hash. Same as the homepage. #}
        <div class="c-hero" data-hero></div>
    </div>
    <div class="c-frontdoor__meta">
        <h1 class="c-frontdoor__title">{{ title }}</h1>
    </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
